# Test: an operator who commits config in the web UI can still edit afterwards
# accept-only: the registered native fixture enforces this scenario's behavioral contract
# VALIDATES: the accepted local credential generation advances only when a local
#            credential changes, so a live break-glass session survives the
#            reload its own commit triggers.
# PREVENTS: the operator losing edit rights inside the request that committed.
#           Every reload advanced the generation, a bare SIGHUP included, and
#           aaa.AuthorizerForResult pins a `ze init` bootstrap admin's session to
#           the generation that authenticated it. The commit succeeded, the
#           commit bar came back read-only, and every later edit answered 403
#           until the operator logged in again.
#
# The session must belong to the zefs admin `ze init` writes, not to a config
# user: only the zefs account carries aaa.ReservedRecoveryProfile, and only a
# session holding that profile is pinned to a generation at all.
#
# The whole sequence is one native fixture because the ordering is the test: the
# second edit must run strictly after the commit, and http= steps run in their
# own phase with no way to place a request after another request's side effect.

option=timeout:value=90s

cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze-test fixture ui/web-recovery-session-survives-commit:timeout=90s
expect=exit:code=0
